#edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497273 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497273 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497273 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497273 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186496659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497273 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "73e9c3a9-039d-5389-8d3a-67841500f704" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497276 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497276 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497276 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497276 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497276 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497276 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497276 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497276 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186496697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497276 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7aa37ed2-9413-56be-8870-8d9839afae71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497299 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497299 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497299 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497299 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186497179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497299 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "beff01d7-6cb0-54e0-bc71-2ce1a1269b34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497303 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497303 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497303 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497303 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497303 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497303 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497303 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497303 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186497160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497303 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cb1457d4-2953-5025-b958-130faff94c81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497330 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497330 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112315" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497330 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497330 #edb/ref 1071 "In a certain country, the number of people $P(t)$ who know a second language, $t$ years after $2005,$ is given by\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\cdot {3}^{t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ was the number of people who knew a second language in $2005.$ If in $2010$ the number of people who knew a second language was $4\\,860\\,000,$ how many people knew a second language in $2005?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497330 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0e669fba-a4ac-508f-acb9-2cbafeb51763" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497330 #edb/ref 1074 "The number of people who knew a second language in $2010$ was $4\\,860\\,000,$ this is $5$ years after $2005.$ Thus, $P(5)=4\\,860\\,000.$\n\nTo find ${P}_{0},$ the number of people who knew a second lenguage in $2005,$  we substitute $t=5$ and $P(5)=4\\,860\\,000$ into the given equation and solve for ${P}_{0},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5) & ={P}_{0}\\cdot {3}^{5} \\\\ 4\\,860\\,000 & ={P}_{0}\\cdot {3}^{5} \\\\ {P}_{0} & =\\frac{4\\,860\\,000}{243} \\\\ {P}_{0} & =20\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in $2005$ there were $20\\,000$ people who knew a second language in that country." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497341 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497341 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112309" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497341 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497341 #edb/ref 1071 "A fungi culture increases by $30%$ each week. If the culture started with $5$ fungi, what equation should we solve to determine how many weeks will it take for the fungi culture to increase to $100?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497341 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "25c45b73-9502-506c-8f02-7a944a17847b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497341 #edb/ref 1074 "The fungi culture $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $30%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=5$ is the initial number of fungi.\n\n- $r=0.3$ is the growth rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in weeks, since the culture started.\n\nSo, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =5\\cdot (1+0.3)^{t} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (1.3)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the number of weeks it takes for the fungi culture to increase to $100,$ we would need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =100 \\\\ 5\\cdot (1.3)^{t} & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497346 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497346 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112257" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497346 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497346 #edb/ref 1071 "A social media influencer had $100$ followers the day she created her account. Since then, the number $F(t)$ of followers after $t$ weeks can be modeled by the function\n\n$$\nF(t)=100\\cdot {3}^{t}.\n$$\n\nHow many weeks will it take for the followers to increase to $72\\,900?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497346 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2f3a7c31-c6fb-510a-b749-2c21cfbaae09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497346 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the time it takes for the number of followers to increase to $72\\,900,$ we need to solve the equation $F(t)=72\\,900$ for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =72\\,900 \\\\ 100\\cdot {3}^{t} & =72\\,900 \\\\ {3}^{t} & =\\frac{72\\,900}{100} \\\\ {3}^{t} & =729 \\\\ {3}^{t} & ={3}^{6} \\\\ t & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number of followers increases to $72\\,900$ after $6$ weeks." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497357 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497357 #edb/ref 1069 "q-61756" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497357 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497357 #edb/ref 1071 "The number $N(t)$ of bees in a hive after $t$ months can be modeled by the function\n\n$$\nN(t)=5\\,000\\cdot {2}^{t}.\n$$\n\nHow many months will it take for the population of bees to increase to $40\\,000?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497357 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "519a949e-8b2c-53c0-bd01-456c0a3f29d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497357 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the time it takes for the number of bees to reach $40\\,000,$ we need to solve the equation $N(t)=40\\,000$ for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =40\\,000 \\\\ 5\\,000\\cdot {2}^{t} & =40\\,000 \\\\ {2}^{t} & =\\frac{40\\,000}{5\\,000} \\\\ {2}^{t} & =\\frac{40}{5} \\\\ {2}^{t} & =8 \\\\ {2}^{t} & ={2}^{3} \\\\ t & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number of bees increases to $40\\,000$ after $3$ months." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497363 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497363 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497363 #edb/ref 1069 "q-237332" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497363 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497363 #edb/ref 1071 "Paul is conducting a scientific experiment with amoebas. He notices that the number of amoebas grows by  $65%$ each day. If there were initially $140$ amoebas, complete the following equation that we should solve to determine how long it will take for the number of amoebas to increase to $800.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\,(${{field-2}}$)^{t}=800$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497363 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "65185e0f-08b4-53fe-970a-61a3c4a92c37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497363 #edb/ref 1074 "The amoeba population $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $65%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=140$ is the initial number of amoebas.\n\n- $r=0.65$ is the growth rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in days, since the beginning of the observation.\n\nSo, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =140\\cdot (1+0.65)^{t} \\\\  & =140\\cdot (1.65)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the time it will take for the amoeba population to increase to $800,$ we would need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =800 \\\\ 140\\cdot (1.65)^{t} & =800.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497370 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497370 #edb/ref 1069 "q-45649" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497370 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497370 #edb/ref 1071 "An economic study in Chile showed that its gross domestic product (GDP) increases fourfold each decade. If the GDP was $$0.5$ billion in $2010$, how many decades will it take for the GDP to increase to $$512$ billion?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497370 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "74b32b7f-8020-5b1d-8650-2271e11205ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497370 #edb/ref 1074 "We know that the GDP increases fourfold each decade. So the GDP, which we'll call $y(t),$ grows exponentially with a growth factor of $4.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny=a{b}^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=0.5$ billion dollars was the GDP in $2010.$\n\n- $b=4$ is the growth factor.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in decades, since $2010.$\n\nTherefore, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=0.5\\cdot {4}^{t}.\n$$\n\nNow, to find the time it takes for the GDP to increase to $$512$ billion, we need to solve the equation $y(t)=512$ for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =512 \\\\ 0.5\\cdot {4}^{t} & =512 \\\\ {4}^{t} & =\\frac{512}{0.5} \\\\ {4}^{t} & =1\\,024 \\\\ {4}^{t} & ={4}^{5} \\\\ t & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, it will take $5$ decades for the GDP to increase to $$512$ billion." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497382 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497382 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112316" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497382 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497382 #edb/ref 1071 "The profits of a store $P(t),$ $t$ years after $2018,$ is given by\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\cdot (1.1)^{t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ was the profit in $2018.$ If the profits in $2020$ were of $$48\\,400,$ what was the profit in $2018?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497382 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8cbabf22-40c3-5c95-8c42-a48a71281e71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497382 #edb/ref 1074 "The store profits in $2020$ were of $$48\\,400,$ this is $2$ years after $2018.$ Thus, $P(2)=48\\,400.$\n\nTo find the profit ${P}_{0}$ in $2018,$  we substitute $t=2$ and $P(2)=48\\,400$ into the given equation and solve for ${P}_{0},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2) & ={P}_{0}\\cdot (1.1)^{2} \\\\ 48\\,400 & ={P}_{0}\\cdot (1.1)^{2} \\\\ {P}_{0} & =\\frac{48\\,400}{1.21} \\\\ {P}_{0} & =40\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profits in $2018$ were of $$40\\,000.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497384 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497384 #edb/ref 1069 "q-237311" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497384 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497384 #edb/ref 1071 "The population of ducks living in a nature reserve triples every year. If there were $8$ ducks initially, how many years will it take for the population to increase to $5\\,832?$\n\nThe population of ducks increases to $5\\,832$ after{{field-1}}years." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497384 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "94ed1bcc-bdbb-5a68-affe-a9e769dd8927" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497384 #edb/ref 1074 "We know that the duck population triples every year. Thus, the duck population, which we will call $y(t),$ grows exponentially with a growth factor of $3.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny=a{b}^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=8$ is the initial population of ducks.\n\n- $b=3$ is the growth factor.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in years, since the beginning of the observation.\n\nTherefore, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=8\\cdot {3}^{t}.\n$$\n\nNow, to find the time it takes for the population of ducks to increase to $5\\,832,$ we need to solve the equation $y(t)=5\\,832$ for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =5\\,832 \\\\ 8\\cdot {3}^{t} & =5\\,832 \\\\ {3}^{t} & =\\frac{5\\,832}{8} \\\\ {3}^{t} & =729 \\\\ {3}^{t} & ={3}^{6} \\\\ t & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population of ducks increases to $5\\,832$ after $6$ years." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
